<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Zicheng rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1630s–<a href="/years/1644">1644</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a> rebellion was a peasant uprising in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> lasting from the 1630s to 1644, fuelled by famine, heavy taxation and the weakness of the Ming state. It culminated in the rebels' capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1644 and the death of the last Ming emperor. The resulting chaos allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> Qing to take control of China.</p></div>
